c-libp2p 23a6172: no close events for a stream that never opened.

libp2p_stream_close emitted STREAM_CLOSED and CONN_CLOSED for every stream
with a host, also for an inbound stream whose protocol was refused ("na"),
which never emitted STREAM_OPENED. The interop listener counts STREAM_OPENED
and STREAM_CLOSED and stops when the count falls to 0 after the ping stream
opened. When a dialer opens a stream the listener refuses and that close
lands after the ping stream opened, the listener stopped in the middle of
the ping, and freed streams that its callback and ping threads still used
(heap use after free, seen with AddressSanitizer).

A stream has a protocol once its negotiation succeeded, so close events are
now emitted only for a stream with a protocol.

diff --git a/src/stream/stream.c b/src/stream/stream.c
index f36b980..54a6f6c 100644
--- a/src/stream/stream.c
+++ b/src/stream/stream.c
@@ -169,8 +169,11 @@ int libp2p_stream_close(libp2p_stream_t *s)
         rc = libp2p_conn_close(st->c);
     else
         rc = LIBP2P_ERR_NULL_PTR;
-    /* Emit stream/conn closed events if we have a host */
-    if (st->host)
+    /* Emit stream/conn closed events if we have a host. A stream with no
+     * protocol never finished negotiation and never emitted STREAM_OPENED, so
+     * it emits no close events either: closing a refused inbound stream must
+     * not look like the close of an open stream. */
+    if (st->host && st->proto)
     {
         {
             libp2p_event_t evt = {0};
